Spec: Undefined pseudo-instruction mnemonics for read-only performance/FPU CSRs allow timing side-channel reads to evade binary audit tools that rely on named-instruction recognition rather than raw CSR-number inspection.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:252-286  macro definition
      CSR_CYCLE (0xc00), CSR_TIME (0xc01), CSR_INSTRET (0xc02) and their high-half counterparts CSR_CYCLEH (0xc80), CSR_TIMEH (0xc81), CSR_INSTRETH (0xc82) are all defined, but the unprivileged-readable FPU status CSRs at 0x001 (fflags), 0x002 (frm), and 0x003 (fcsr) have zero corresponding macros anywhere in the tree. The RISC-V ISA defines pseudo-instructions rdfflags/frflags (→ 0x001), frrm/rdfrm (→0x002), and frcsr/rdfcsr (→0x003) that resolve to these addresses. Static-analysis tools scanning for CSR_* macro references will correctly identify cycle/time/instret reads as potential timing side-channels, but have no symbolic anchor to detect fflags/frm/fcsr reads. This creates an asymmetric audit gap where timing-gadget code can use the raw 'csrr rd, fcsr' mnemonic or the rdfflags pseudo-instruction to leak FPU state timing without triggering macro-based detection signatures.
